00:25:00.130 --> 00:25:02.210
that really started, that really kinda

00:25:02.210 --> 00:25:03.670
hit its peak in the 80s,

00:25:03.670 --> 00:25:07.770
when parents started really
worrying about Satanism,

00:25:07.770 --> 00:25:10.783
I mean we remember very very clearly,

00:25:13.069 --> 00:25:14.500
there was a whole lot of moral panic

00:25:14.500 --> 00:25:18.453
around people actually worshiping Satan.

00:25:19.836 --> 00:25:21.867
We actually were told that "No, you guys,

00:25:21.867 --> 00:25:23.760
"people are worshiping Satan."

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:26.360
Never mind the fact that
the whole Church of Satan

00:25:26.360 --> 00:25:29.150
was started as almost a mockery

00:25:29.150 --> 00:25:31.711
of the idea of religion in general.

00:25:31.711 --> 00:25:35.047
I remember thinking as a kid, I was like,

00:25:35.047 --> 00:25:38.057
"So you're telling me
that there are people

00:25:38.057 --> 00:25:40.797
"who worship Satan, which means

00:25:40.797 --> 00:25:42.777
"that they believe in the framework

00:25:42.777 --> 00:25:44.847
"that there's a God and there's a Satan,

00:25:44.847 --> 00:25:47.020
"and they're choosing the bad guy."

00:25:47.020 --> 00:25:50.600
This is illogical on so
many different levels.

00:25:50.600 --> 00:25:52.160
'Cause you're buying into the worldview

00:25:52.160 --> 00:25:54.323
that includes Satan and then choosing him.

00:25:55.299 --> 00:25:59.360
That's just, I'm not saying
that no one does that,

00:25:59.360 --> 00:26:02.260
but it's not a societal
problem, and it never was.

00:26:02.260 --> 00:26:04.117
We knew enough bad kids to be like

00:26:04.117 --> 00:26:05.273
"Oh, that's a bad kid,

00:26:06.632 --> 00:26:08.582
"I bet he would like to worship Satan."

00:26:09.540 --> 00:26:11.300
He's not saying that he does, but.

00:26:11.300 --> 00:26:13.839
And then we were told that if you played

00:26:13.839 --> 00:26:17.220
all this rock music
backwards, backmasting,

00:26:17.220 --> 00:26:18.464
we would literally go--

00:26:18.464 --> 00:26:19.320
Backmasking.

00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:20.870
Masking, yeah, we would literally go

00:26:20.870 --> 00:26:23.140
to these presentations where
these guys would get up

00:26:23.140 --> 00:26:26.210
and they would play
Led Zeppelin and AC/DC,

00:26:26.210 --> 00:26:28.040
and other bands backwards,

00:26:28.040 --> 00:26:30.060
and show all the Satanic messages.

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:31.593
Whose power is Satan?

00:26:32.840 --> 00:26:35.993
That's what Robert Plant
sang in reverse on a song.

00:26:36.970 --> 00:26:39.490
And I remember that being
a film from the 70s,

00:26:39.490 --> 00:26:43.380
it seemed like a 70s-ish film

00:26:43.380 --> 00:26:48.380
that was then screened again
in the early early 90s for us,

00:26:49.360 --> 00:26:50.880
at Campbell University.

00:26:50.880 --> 00:26:53.140
Was a little bit behind.

00:26:53.140 --> 00:26:55.900
Well it was, they were still trying

00:26:55.900 --> 00:26:59.100
to eradicate the, Led
Zeppelin was still popular.

00:26:59.100 --> 00:27:02.090
But I think this is,
what I'm pointing at is,

00:27:02.090 --> 00:27:04.910
in line with what you were
saying before, which is,

00:27:04.910 --> 00:27:08.810
what an effective way
to manipulate people,

00:27:08.810 --> 00:27:11.730
and manipulate their
behavior, is to understand

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:14.940
what their greatest fears are,
and then you can basically

00:27:14.940 --> 00:27:17.660
just begin selling them any
kind of bullshit you want to.

00:27:17.660 --> 00:27:20.290
Especially within the, if it works

00:27:20.290 --> 00:27:22.540
within the framework of
what they already believe.

00:27:22.540 --> 00:27:25.240
Exactly, so I'm saying, if you tell people

00:27:25.240 --> 00:27:28.210
that rock music is actually

00:27:28.210 --> 00:27:30.613
a subversive way to worship Satan,

00:27:34.200 --> 00:27:35.860
and that's consistent with the fears

00:27:35.860 --> 00:27:37.773
that they already have about it,

00:27:37.773 --> 00:27:40.300
then you can get them
to believe things like,

00:27:40.300 --> 00:27:42.580
people are intentionally
putting Satanic messages

00:27:42.580 --> 00:27:45.810
into their song, because they
legitimately worship Satan.

00:27:45.810 --> 00:27:47.170
If anyone actually ever did that,

00:27:47.170 --> 00:27:48.670
it was because they were playing into it

00:27:48.670 --> 00:27:49.940
in a tongue in cheek way,

00:27:49.940 --> 00:27:54.150
but anyway, so Whitewood is in a community

00:27:54.150 --> 00:27:56.690
where this is the way people think,

00:27:56.690 --> 00:27:58.937
and he's like "This is
what my experience is,

00:27:58.937 --> 00:28:01.447
"and these are the kids that I need

00:28:01.447 --> 00:28:03.630
"in order to accomplish my mission."

00:28:03.630 --> 00:28:06.400
So you do that mathematical equation

00:28:06.400 --> 00:28:09.090
and you kinda come up with a
really strict reform school

00:28:09.090 --> 00:28:11.160
that the kids have to live at,

00:28:11.160 --> 00:28:12.743
so you gotta have control.

00:28:14.070 --> 00:28:15.610
And then of course you've
eventually gotta get

00:28:15.610 --> 00:28:17.450
some other adults to help you out,

00:28:17.450 --> 00:28:19.510
and you've gotta manipulate them as well.

00:28:19.510 --> 00:28:22.380
And the way you do that is twofold.

00:28:22.380 --> 00:28:27.380
One, you play a mean organ at the church.

00:28:27.770 --> 00:28:30.880
I mean, it's hard to argue with that.

00:28:30.880 --> 00:28:35.720
He's there every Sunday,
playing that organ, you know?

00:28:35.720 --> 00:28:37.263
That means a lot.

00:28:39.810 --> 00:28:42.350
He might have made a
run at being a pastor,

00:28:42.350 --> 00:28:44.203
but that takes a lot more time.

00:28:45.370 --> 00:28:48.560
And then the second thing is,

00:28:48.560 --> 00:28:53.560
you master that pit barbecue,
and you win 'em over.

00:28:53.610 --> 00:28:56.460
You win 'em over.

00:28:56.460 --> 00:28:58.170
People can overlook a world of hurt,

00:28:58.170 --> 00:29:03.170
literally, they overlooked deaths,

00:29:03.480 --> 00:29:05.210
which is something that we wrestled with,

00:29:05.210 --> 00:29:07.607
'cause it's like "Man,
once you have kids dying,

00:29:07.607 --> 00:29:12.607
"is this town really gonna let
this school just continue?"

00:29:12.630 --> 00:29:13.990
Well, but that happens all the time.

00:29:13.990 --> 00:29:18.093
I mean, in my mind, it's
not that big of a leap.

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:24.080
People let injustice fester,
especially when the person

00:29:25.670 --> 00:29:29.460
who's in charge of it seems legitimate,

00:29:29.460 --> 00:29:31.630
and there seems to be a
legitimate explanation,

00:29:31.630 --> 00:29:34.187
and it's like "Oh, okay,
three kids over a decade

00:29:34.187 --> 00:29:36.227
"have died in accidents at this place."

00:29:37.460 --> 00:29:40.830
And this is largely based
on, so the reform school

00:29:40.830 --> 00:29:45.760
in the book, we had the
whole thing sketched out,

00:29:45.760 --> 00:29:48.580
but then we actually, once we
had outlined the entire book,

00:29:48.580 --> 00:29:51.350
we found out about this reform school.

00:29:51.350 --> 00:29:52.940
Yeah, Lance found this article

00:29:52.940 --> 00:29:54.830
and sent it to us.
Somewhere in the northeast,

00:29:54.830 --> 00:29:58.580
that even up until,
within the last decade,

00:29:58.580 --> 00:30:00.890
had operated, and they had
found that they were doing

00:30:00.890 --> 00:30:05.100
all kinds of crazy disciplinary
things on the kids,

00:30:05.100 --> 00:30:08.540
including rolling them up in a carpet.

00:30:08.540 --> 00:30:12.603
Yep, because we had written,

00:30:14.190 --> 00:30:16.030
let's see, we, I'm trying to figure out

00:30:16.030 --> 00:30:18.847
if we knew or if we changed
it, but there was a box.

00:30:19.792 --> 00:30:23.510
We had not decided what the specific

00:30:23.510 --> 00:30:24.980
disciplinary thing was, but we knew

00:30:24.980 --> 00:30:26.730
we wanted it to be something
where kids were isolated,

00:30:26.730 --> 00:30:29.600
and we wanted it to seem
kinda like the carpet thing,

00:30:29.600 --> 00:30:34.510
so yeah, the first draft, it was the box,

00:30:34.510 --> 00:30:38.140
and it was a wooden box that
was about two and a half feet

00:30:38.140 --> 00:30:42.110
on each side, and you basically
sort of ball a kid up,

00:30:42.110 --> 00:30:43.273
and you'd put them in this dark box

00:30:43.273 --> 00:30:45.340
that had some breathing holes,

00:30:45.340 --> 00:30:47.520
and then the guy would come
in and kinda kick it around,

00:30:47.520 --> 00:30:48.830
and you would end up upside down

00:30:48.830 --> 00:30:49.930
and you couldn't get turned,

00:30:49.930 --> 00:30:51.980
or it's too small to turn around inside.

00:30:51.980 --> 00:30:52.960
But I just thought that,

00:30:52.960 --> 00:30:56.390
there's just nothing, it
just wasn't as memorable

00:30:56.390 --> 00:30:59.916
or as visceral as the roll.

00:30:59.916 --> 00:31:03.180
So the carpet roll is a
real thing they did to kids,

00:31:03.180 --> 00:31:07.710
and these kids at a certain,
once they reached adulthood,

00:31:07.710 --> 00:31:09.860
if I remember correctly,
a lot of them were

00:31:11.180 --> 00:31:13.080
troubled, it had residual effects,

00:31:13.080 --> 00:31:15.450
and there was a lot of suicide.

00:31:15.450 --> 00:31:16.580
A lot of them were dying,

00:31:16.580 --> 00:31:20.080
and then there was an investigation,

00:31:20.080 --> 00:31:24.110
through Facebook, "Whatever
happened to my classmates,"

00:31:24.110 --> 00:31:26.920
and then it turns out a lot
of 'em had committed suicide.

00:31:26.920 --> 00:31:29.100
Yeah, I don't remember that detail,

00:31:29.100 --> 00:31:32.350
but there was a lot of bad
stuff going on at the time,

00:31:32.350 --> 00:31:35.050
and people didn't, they raised an eyebrow,

00:31:35.050 --> 00:31:37.389
but it didn't cause any action.

00:31:37.389 --> 00:31:39.843
So it's sad that these things do happen.

00:31:41.010 --> 00:31:45.920
A related question, Shell, thevelvethook,

00:31:45.920 --> 00:31:50.920
who's changed the moniker
to include scooter leg.

00:31:51.580 --> 00:31:52.430
That's an homage.

00:31:54.683 --> 00:31:56.967
"You mentioned the seven pointed star

00:31:56.967 --> 00:31:58.787
"being significant, in Christianity,

00:31:58.787 --> 00:32:00.227
"the seven pointed star represents

00:32:00.227 --> 00:32:03.017
"the seven gifts of the
spirit, wisdom, understanding,

00:32:03.017 --> 00:32:05.977
"council, might, knowledge,
fear, and delight."

00:32:08.550 --> 00:32:11.730
Those aren't the gifts of
the spirit that I know.

00:32:11.730 --> 00:32:13.157
Yeah, that must be NIV.

00:32:17.610 --> 00:32:19.840
It might be the message.

00:32:19.840 --> 00:32:21.090
Yeah, it must be.

00:32:21.090 --> 00:32:23.937
That's some biblical
translation humor for you.

00:32:23.937 --> 00:32:25.667
"So I wondered if this was a metaphor

00:32:25.667 --> 00:32:28.880
"for the seven lost causes in some way."

00:32:28.880 --> 00:32:31.670
Absolutely, the seven pointed star

00:32:31.670 --> 00:32:36.670
is a metaphor for the
lost causes that the cult

00:32:36.730 --> 00:32:38.620
needs to get into the spring.

00:32:38.620 --> 00:32:41.350
Well, and lemme pair this
with this next question, too,

00:32:41.350 --> 00:32:43.547
from Phanto_Moose, who asked

00:32:43.547 --> 00:32:45.577
"Can you elaborate on the
Latin used by the cult?

00:32:45.577 --> 00:32:49.417
"First one seems to be vita
est aqua, life is water,

00:32:49.417 --> 00:32:51.277
"but couldn't completely
figure out the second one,

00:32:51.277 --> 00:32:54.707
"electus in-trot aquam sanctum,
selected in holy water?"

00:32:57.010 --> 00:33:02.010
Yeah, so all the stuff that
is true about the cult,

00:33:03.740 --> 00:33:07.530
like the robes, the seven pointed star,

00:33:07.530 --> 00:33:10.460
the Latin chants that are happening,

00:33:10.460 --> 00:33:13.700
just to kinda get your
mind in the right place,

00:33:13.700 --> 00:33:17.720
all of this was fabricated by Whitewood

00:33:17.720 --> 00:33:22.050
as a manipulative scheme
to give the appearances

00:33:22.050 --> 00:33:23.260
and trappings of a cult,

00:33:23.260 --> 00:33:25.930
or some sort of religious organization

00:33:25.930 --> 00:33:27.410
that these people would buy into.

00:33:27.410 --> 00:33:28.243
So it isn't--

00:33:29.120 --> 00:33:31.803
The Keeper didn't transfer this knowledge

00:33:34.480 --> 00:33:38.740
and this symbology to Whitewood,

00:33:38.740 --> 00:33:40.047
you know what the Keeper wants,

00:33:40.047 --> 00:33:43.900
he wants seven lost causes.

00:33:43.900 --> 00:33:48.900
He wants kids of a certain age
with a certain oomph to them,

00:33:50.890 --> 00:33:54.790
because there's, his fuel
is associated with that.

00:33:54.790 --> 00:33:58.120
But everything else is
Whitewood's problem.

00:33:58.120 --> 00:33:59.740
Right, and the seven pointed star,

00:33:59.740 --> 00:34:03.023
yes, it was chosen because
the Keeper told him,

00:34:03.865 --> 00:34:05.307
"You gotta get seven kids."

00:34:06.880 --> 00:34:08.540
And while the seven pointed star

00:34:08.540 --> 00:34:10.090
does have significance in Christianity,

00:34:10.090 --> 00:34:12.600
it also, like most symbols,

00:34:12.600 --> 00:34:14.260
it has significance in other places

00:34:14.260 --> 00:34:16.400
including Pagan religion,

00:34:16.400 --> 00:34:20.360
and we just took it and
turned it upside down

00:34:20.360 --> 00:34:22.860
so that the predominant arrow--

00:34:22.860 --> 00:34:25.013
Let's say Whitewood turned it upside down.

00:34:26.070 --> 00:34:29.790
Was pointing down towards
the Earth, towards water.

00:34:29.790 --> 00:34:32.740
Also, the triangle in general in Paganism

00:34:32.740 --> 00:34:35.513
is a symbol of water,
'cause it's pointing down.

00:34:35.513 --> 00:34:39.573
It's also a symbol of feminism
because of the triangle.

00:34:41.160 --> 00:34:42.580
What's that got to do with this?

00:34:42.580 --> 00:34:44.358
I'm just letting you know.

00:34:44.358 --> 00:34:46.690
It wasn't a joke, I'm
just letting you know.

00:34:46.690 --> 00:34:51.690
Now, the Latin specifically, so again,

00:34:52.240 --> 00:34:55.260
my thought process behind this was,

00:34:55.260 --> 00:35:00.220
so, when we were writing
the section about the cult,

00:35:00.220 --> 00:35:02.210
I was like "Okay, well
what could they chant,"

00:35:02.210 --> 00:35:03.957
and I was like "All right, well,
what if there was something

00:35:03.957 --> 00:35:06.767
"that was like life is
water or water is life,

00:35:06.767 --> 00:35:10.307
"and then once they get down
there and they're selecting

00:35:10.307 --> 00:35:13.497
"the specific person to go into,

00:35:13.497 --> 00:35:14.527
"put their hand into the water,

00:35:14.527 --> 00:35:16.577
"it'll be the selected one,

00:35:16.577 --> 00:35:20.460
"or the chosen one enters the water,"

00:35:20.460 --> 00:35:22.070
and I can't remember whether
or not the chosen one

00:35:22.070 --> 00:35:24.970
refers to the person
chosen in sort of the weird

00:35:24.970 --> 00:35:27.370
duck duck goose game, or
if it's Whitewood himself,

00:35:27.370 --> 00:35:30.490
but anyway, it was very simply

00:35:31.340 --> 00:35:34.600
putting the meaning into Google Translate,

00:35:34.600 --> 00:35:38.370
and going from English to
Latin, until we got something

00:35:38.370 --> 00:35:42.680
that, "Oh, that kinda seems
like it could be a cool chant."

00:35:42.680 --> 00:35:44.957
And also, I was thinking at the time,

00:35:44.957 --> 00:35:46.957
"We're probably gonna get
a couple of things wrong,

00:35:46.957 --> 00:35:48.687
"maybe the tense is wrong,

00:35:48.687 --> 00:35:51.037
"maybe this isn't a perfect translation,

00:35:51.037 --> 00:35:52.847
"but let's not pay too
much attention to it

00:35:52.847 --> 00:35:54.517
"because we're trying to make it seem like

00:35:54.517 --> 00:35:57.677
"this dude who has no
experience in any of this stuff

00:35:57.677 --> 00:36:00.707
"is going to the library
and kinda coming up

00:36:00.707 --> 00:36:04.600
"with this language that
wouldn't be perfect."

00:36:04.600 --> 00:36:07.700
The system would not be this
perfectly cohesive system

00:36:07.700 --> 00:36:10.520
that feels like it has some
sort of supernatural force,

00:36:10.520 --> 00:36:12.810
but it's more like
somebody creating something

00:36:12.810 --> 00:36:14.740
to fool people, and it's gonna

00:36:14.740 --> 00:36:16.220
have some inconsistencies in it,

00:36:16.220 --> 00:36:20.300
so "I chose this, and
this is what you chant."

00:36:20.300 --> 00:36:23.140
So everything has
significance but it's not,

00:36:23.140 --> 00:36:25.550
it doesn't have metaphysical significance,

00:36:25.550 --> 00:36:26.383
it's just--

00:36:26.383 --> 00:36:28.047
He made it up to make
sense and to manipulate,

00:36:28.047 --> 00:36:32.760
you know kinda like, you think
I was gonna throw another

00:36:32.760 --> 00:36:36.010
religion or cult under
the bus, didn't you?

00:36:36.010 --> 00:36:40.692
So yeah, so the Latin, and the symbology,

00:36:40.692 --> 00:36:44.830
the star, the freaking star's
on the side of the book.

00:36:44.830 --> 00:36:49.830
Look at that, Whitewood
tainted the spine of our novel.

00:36:52.080 --> 00:36:54.533
'Cause there's the star,
that means nothing.

00:36:55.790 --> 00:37:00.638
That's a deception, man,
we got it right there

00:37:00.638 --> 00:37:03.680
on the spine of the book,
'cause it looks cool.

00:37:03.680 --> 00:37:05.090
But it means something now.

00:37:05.090 --> 00:37:07.450
It means the lost, yeah, it means...

00:37:07.450 --> 00:37:08.760
Or else I shouldn't have got it tattooed

00:37:08.760 --> 00:37:10.470
on my left butt cheek.

00:37:10.470 --> 00:37:11.303
Oh.

00:37:14.370 --> 00:37:16.790
So yeah, he made all that stuff up, y'all.

00:37:16.790 --> 00:37:19.480
He made it all up, so then we go back

00:37:19.480 --> 00:37:23.690
to facts like, why would a guy
go through all this trouble,

00:37:23.690 --> 00:37:27.460
and so not to make, I mean yeah,

00:37:27.460 --> 00:37:29.410
I think you do feel for him,

00:37:29.410 --> 00:37:31.340
but it doesn't justify his actions,

00:37:31.340 --> 00:37:34.440
I mean would you wanna do
anything to save your daughter,

00:37:34.440 --> 00:37:39.440
to remove your daughter's
pain, to give her a full life,

00:37:40.890 --> 00:37:45.013
well, hopefully not
anything, hopefully not this,

00:37:46.590 --> 00:37:50.560
but I mean it's not just the one decision,

00:37:50.560 --> 00:37:54.670
he's continually communing
with his daughter,

00:37:54.670 --> 00:37:56.493
who's frozen in time.

00:37:59.090 --> 00:38:01.050
And every time he goes down there

00:38:01.050 --> 00:38:02.680
his heart is wrenched one more time,

00:38:02.680 --> 00:38:04.680
so it's like, at a certain point,

00:38:04.680 --> 00:38:08.550
he's kind of, again, I'm
not trying to justify this,

00:38:08.550 --> 00:38:13.550
but psychologically, you can go crazy

00:38:13.820 --> 00:38:16.520
trying to care for somebody,

00:38:16.520 --> 00:38:18.200
if you've reached a total dead end,

00:38:18.200 --> 00:38:20.317
and especially if it's
like "This is the answer,

00:38:20.317 --> 00:38:22.017
"if you just do this."

00:38:23.700 --> 00:38:26.313
You can find yourself
becoming convinced of,

00:38:27.638 --> 00:38:30.423
or justifying your actions.

00:38:31.370 --> 00:38:33.500
Yeah, so we wanted it to be

00:38:33.500 --> 00:38:35.480
a little bit messy and complicated,

00:38:35.480 --> 00:38:38.203
but ultimately, you're still,

00:38:39.879 --> 00:38:42.380
I think if you're normal,
you're still gonna conclude

00:38:42.380 --> 00:38:43.500
that none of the stuff

00:38:43.500 --> 00:38:45.030
that he's doing is actually justified,

00:38:45.030 --> 00:38:47.720
it's just he has his own reasons which may

00:38:47.720 --> 00:38:49.890
draw a little bit of sympathy from you,

00:38:49.890 --> 00:38:53.180
but not to the point that you
think any of it's justified.

00:38:53.180 --> 00:38:55.250
Just think, I mean
you've got this poor girl

00:38:55.250 --> 00:38:58.050
who doesn't wanna get out of
her dress getting in there,

00:39:00.057 --> 00:39:02.410
"Oh my lord," Wayne
said, "It was working,"

00:39:02.410 --> 00:39:04.200
the water all around them began to bubble,

00:39:04.200 --> 00:39:06.610
Wayne began to laugh,
unconcerned about disturbing

00:39:06.610 --> 00:39:08.150
anyone who might be within earshot.

00:39:08.150 --> 00:39:10.560
Ruby laughed too, "This is really fun."

00:39:10.560 --> 00:39:12.850
She pushed off the
bottom, beginning to swim.

00:39:12.850 --> 00:39:15.160
Wayne was careful not
to let go of her hand.

00:39:15.160 --> 00:39:17.780
That didn't matter, though, as
soon as she dropped her face

00:39:17.780 --> 00:39:20.220
below the surface, Ruby was violently

00:39:20.220 --> 00:39:21.920
sucked down into the water.

00:39:21.920 --> 00:39:23.670
Her hand slipped from her father's.

00:39:24.827 --> 00:39:27.377
"Ruby!" He yelled, "Baby!"

00:39:29.840 --> 00:39:31.123
Then he got spit out.

00:39:34.390 --> 00:39:36.773
Moving along, this is a question,

00:39:38.640 --> 00:39:42.847
a quick one related to the
spring, Joel McCray asks,

00:39:42.847 --> 00:39:46.903
"Are the seven souls
taken by the Keeper dead?

00:39:48.477 --> 00:39:50.747
"What happened to them,
do they go to an afterlife

00:39:50.747 --> 00:39:51.947
"or just not exist?"

00:39:54.950 --> 00:39:59.950
So no, the seven souls that are in there

00:40:02.190 --> 00:40:05.550
are in the same situation that Rex,

00:40:05.550 --> 00:40:10.323
I mean that Leaf and
Alicia were, in this book.

00:40:11.350 --> 00:40:16.350
So, in this state of stasis,

00:40:16.410 --> 00:40:20.373
which your body is not aging.

00:40:23.520 --> 00:40:25.380
There's some things happening
in your mind, of course,

00:40:25.380 --> 00:40:27.430
the longer that you're
there, it seems like the less

00:40:27.430 --> 00:40:29.860
you can kinda remember and hold onto

00:40:29.860 --> 00:40:31.513
the life that you had before.

00:40:32.500 --> 00:40:37.050
And interestingly, this,
I don't wanna get into

00:40:37.050 --> 00:40:39.250
too much into this, because I don't like

00:40:39.250 --> 00:40:42.010
taking so much of the mystery,

00:40:42.010 --> 00:40:45.690
any of the mystery out,
but I will say that

00:40:45.690 --> 00:40:48.960
the nature of the spring and
the Keeper of the spring,

00:40:48.960 --> 00:40:52.230
and even the nature of this, the void

00:40:52.230 --> 00:40:54.890
and the people sort of
being suspended in time,

00:40:54.890 --> 00:40:56.513
and kinda trapped in this place,

00:40:57.804 --> 00:41:01.730
every element of that is at
least somewhat influenced

00:41:01.730 --> 00:41:06.730
by Celtic mythology, right,

00:41:06.910 --> 00:41:08.473
and that's why in the book,

00:41:09.610 --> 00:41:12.810
they break out the giant
book that talks about,

00:41:12.810 --> 00:41:14.953
I can't remember the
name of the book, but,

00:41:16.240 --> 00:41:18.350
that's when they get some information

00:41:18.350 --> 00:41:19.700
about the nature of the spring

00:41:19.700 --> 00:41:21.070
and the blood and all this stuff,

00:41:21.070 --> 00:41:22.470
and the blood being the key,

00:41:23.390 --> 00:41:28.390
that's because, some of the
particular choices that we made

00:41:28.390 --> 00:41:30.450
are just things that we came up with,

00:41:30.450 --> 00:41:34.110
but the general idea of there being this

00:41:34.110 --> 00:41:39.110
sort of being in charge of
a spring that represents

00:41:41.040 --> 00:41:45.280
almost a portal to a slightly
different plane of existence,

00:41:45.280 --> 00:41:48.180
these are concepts that
come up time and time again

00:41:48.180 --> 00:41:49.453
in Celtic mythology,

00:41:50.580 --> 00:41:52.240
which in a second we'll,

00:41:52.240 --> 00:41:53.630
when we get to the end, we'll talk about

00:41:53.630 --> 00:41:56.640
what does this mean about
a potential second book,

00:41:56.640 --> 00:41:59.113
and where else could this go,

00:41:59.970 --> 00:42:01.940
but I will say that all of that is,

00:42:01.940 --> 00:42:04.320
it's not completely fabricated,

00:42:04.320 --> 00:42:05.650
we didn't just invent it out of thin air,

00:42:05.650 --> 00:42:08.150
we kinda looked and did some research on

00:42:08.150 --> 00:42:09.540
the way people thought about that,

00:42:09.540 --> 00:42:12.410
in sort of ancient Celtic times,

00:42:12.410 --> 00:42:15.280
and used that to influence us.

00:42:15.280 --> 00:42:18.890
So no, long story short,
or long answer short,

00:42:18.890 --> 00:42:22.370
those people are not dead,
they're still in the void.

00:42:22.370 --> 00:42:23.970
You mentioned when we were
talking about Whitewood,

00:42:23.970 --> 00:42:27.487
you said he would come
up with the symbology

00:42:27.487 --> 00:42:29.827
and the Latin and stuff
like he went to the library

00:42:29.827 --> 00:42:33.123
and was doing research, which
reminds me of this question.

00:42:34.757 --> 00:42:39.467
Jade_iemrg, "Is the man who told Janine

00:42:39.467 --> 00:42:42.147
"in the library basement about the deaths

00:42:42.147 --> 00:42:45.147
"at Whitewood school going
to be talked about at all?

00:42:45.147 --> 00:42:47.767
"He disappeared, and I have
a feeling his character

00:42:47.767 --> 00:42:50.150
"has a story to be told!"

00:42:50.150 --> 00:42:53.470
Yeah, we're working on
a spinoff novel series

00:42:53.470 --> 00:42:57.330
about library dude, creepy library dude.

00:42:57.330 --> 00:43:01.180
Well first of all, you
ever go to the library?

00:43:01.180 --> 00:43:03.850
There's always somebody who, you can tell

00:43:03.850 --> 00:43:05.893
they spent a lot of time at the library.

00:43:08.120 --> 00:43:11.720
So, maybe Whitewood went there.

00:43:11.720 --> 00:43:13.890
Janine definitely went there for answers,

00:43:13.890 --> 00:43:18.890
and this guy apparently knew
what she was looking for,

00:43:21.380 --> 00:43:23.800
and had a little information, but then,

00:43:23.800 --> 00:43:27.500
either he was a ghost, or he just ghosted.

00:43:27.500 --> 00:43:29.100
I mean he wanted to help, but...

00:43:33.270 --> 00:43:34.410
Do you have a straightforward answer,

00:43:34.410 --> 00:43:37.190
because I have a roundabout answer.

00:43:37.190 --> 00:43:38.050
Who is this guy?

00:43:38.050 --> 00:43:42.630
Well, go ahead.

00:43:42.630 --> 00:43:45.798
I just think that in this town,

00:43:45.798 --> 00:43:47.560
you can't expect everyone to believe

00:43:47.560 --> 00:43:50.110
exactly the same thing,
for there not to be anybody

00:43:50.110 --> 00:43:53.616
who's more suspicious, so to me,

00:43:53.616 --> 00:43:57.990
it rang true that
there's at least somebody

00:43:57.990 --> 00:44:00.193
that you encounter in the story that,

00:44:01.590 --> 00:44:03.620
you're not taking this
hook, line, and sinker.

00:44:03.620 --> 00:44:04.453
Somebody who's a little bit

00:44:04.453 --> 00:44:06.570
of a conspiracy theorist.

00:44:06.570 --> 00:44:09.000
But every once in a while,
conspiracy theorists are right.

00:44:09.000 --> 00:44:10.893
But then not accepted, kinda like,

00:44:13.130 --> 00:44:16.470
we say conspiracy theorist with derision,

00:44:16.470 --> 00:44:20.747
and I'm sure in the town, any nay-sayer

00:44:20.747 --> 00:44:23.310
would be, who knows what they'd be

00:44:24.400 --> 00:44:26.583
scratching on the side of his car?

00:44:27.950 --> 00:44:30.207
So it's like, "Hey, I'ma
help you out a little bit,

00:44:30.207 --> 00:44:33.117
"but I don't wanna be
associated with this."

00:44:34.070 --> 00:44:37.030
Is the simple explanation.

00:44:37.030 --> 00:44:38.927
Who is he and how much
does he really know?

00:44:38.927 --> 00:44:41.470
And I would say, an
even simpler explanation

00:44:42.380 --> 00:44:47.070
that doesn't even, it's very much,

00:44:47.070 --> 00:44:49.930
all right, she needs to
do some kind of research

00:44:49.930 --> 00:44:52.176
and she needs, again, this
is one of those things

00:44:52.176 --> 00:44:54.900
that in storytelling it's like okay, well,

00:44:54.900 --> 00:44:57.750
Janine needs to learn something

00:44:57.750 --> 00:45:01.270
that the audience needs to also know

00:45:01.270 --> 00:45:03.070
in order to move this story forward.

00:45:03.070 --> 00:45:04.510
Where would you get this
type of information,

00:45:04.510 --> 00:45:06.640
well you would get it at a library.

00:45:06.640 --> 00:45:09.320
What kind of town is
Bleak Creek, and also,

00:45:09.320 --> 00:45:12.000
what kind of story are we trying to tell?

00:45:12.000 --> 00:45:14.490
Okay, so I think the library scene

00:45:14.490 --> 00:45:17.590
is actually pretty
indicative of exactly the way

00:45:17.590 --> 00:45:18.740
we wanted to write this book,

00:45:18.740 --> 00:45:20.860
so the first thing that happens is,

00:45:20.860 --> 00:45:23.130
okay, something ironically funny.

00:45:23.130 --> 00:45:26.670
There's a loud librarian, it's a simple,

00:45:26.670 --> 00:45:30.123
simple joke that we got a
good bit of mileage out of.

00:45:31.105 --> 00:45:34.290
And then, oh, but this is
also a scary, scary thing,

00:45:34.290 --> 00:45:36.900
so she's gonna go down into this basement,

00:45:36.900 --> 00:45:38.840
it's gonna get a little
bit weird when she says

00:45:38.840 --> 00:45:41.600
that she's asking about
the Whitewood school,

00:45:41.600 --> 00:45:45.910
and what better way to
introduce a little bit of,

00:45:45.910 --> 00:45:47.500
I mean she obviously passes this guy

00:45:47.500 --> 00:45:49.240
who's kinda following her with his eyes

00:45:49.240 --> 00:45:51.900
as she goes down and walks
through the main part

00:45:51.900 --> 00:45:54.020
of the library to then go to the basement,

00:45:54.020 --> 00:45:55.590
and then what if all of a sudden he's just

00:45:55.590 --> 00:45:58.350
right behind her, not necessarily

00:45:58.350 --> 00:46:00.070
because there is significance to this guy

00:46:00.070 --> 00:46:01.880
that will be explored later,

00:46:01.880 --> 00:46:04.933
but mostly because it's
just really creepy.

00:46:05.960 --> 00:46:07.570
Because it's a more inventive way

00:46:07.570 --> 00:46:08.610
to get this information.
You know that she's

00:46:08.610 --> 00:46:12.413
being watched, you know that she's being,

00:46:13.880 --> 00:46:16.410
there's a lot, there's
increasing attention

00:46:16.410 --> 00:46:20.420
on Janine's efforts, and so how far

00:46:20.420 --> 00:46:23.533
is this gonna go, is she gonna be harmed?

00:46:24.830 --> 00:46:28.070
And so, hopefully you're thinking that

00:46:28.070 --> 00:46:29.220
as you're reading this,

00:46:30.330 --> 00:46:33.030
I mean she's lying about the recipes

00:46:33.030 --> 00:46:35.773
and that's kinda not going too great.

00:46:37.640 --> 00:46:40.883
She kinda, I really enjoy her lies,

00:46:45.010 --> 00:46:48.747
but I'm hoping that the reader's like

00:46:48.747 --> 00:46:50.477
"Okay, I just don't know
if that lie's good enough,

00:46:50.477 --> 00:46:53.600
"I think she might be putting
a target on her back."

00:46:53.600 --> 00:46:56.103
Or like I said, on her car, at least.

00:46:57.539 --> 00:46:59.320
So then what's this guy gonna do?

00:46:59.320 --> 00:47:00.570
And the funny thing is,

00:47:03.970 --> 00:47:05.770
it's obvious by the way we ended the book,

00:47:05.770 --> 00:47:07.820
which somebody pointed out in a question,

00:47:09.310 --> 00:47:12.240
that we want the story to continue.

00:47:12.240 --> 00:47:15.063
The book ends with, okay, Ruby's out,

00:47:18.570 --> 00:47:21.010
and this is not quite reconciled, I mean,

00:47:21.010 --> 00:47:23.830
no justice has been done to

00:47:23.830 --> 00:47:25.350
what these adults have been doing,

00:47:25.350 --> 00:47:28.160
and what's going on in the
spring, and there's still people

00:47:28.160 --> 00:47:30.603
that we love and care
about who are stuck there.

00:47:31.930 --> 00:47:34.680
Is Alicia gonna get back to normal,

00:47:34.680 --> 00:47:36.390
there's a lot of open
questions, and that was

00:47:36.390 --> 00:47:38.930
very very intentional, and I think that

00:47:38.930 --> 00:47:42.010
one of the interesting
things that we're finding,

00:47:42.010 --> 00:47:44.543
is when you ask this
question about this man,

00:47:46.120 --> 00:47:48.840
well I just told you, no, there
was no greater significance

00:47:48.840 --> 00:47:51.570
to this guy, he represents an opportunity

00:47:51.570 --> 00:47:54.770
to make the scene scary
and he's also indicative

00:47:54.770 --> 00:47:57.920
of just the nature of the
town, and like Link said,

00:47:57.920 --> 00:47:58.753
that there's gonna be people

00:47:58.753 --> 00:48:00.410
who are skeptical about this school.

00:48:01.270 --> 00:48:06.270
But, the cool thing about
potentially writing a second book,

00:48:07.600 --> 00:48:10.620
is if we wanted there to be
significance to that guy,

00:48:10.620 --> 00:48:13.230
and we wanted to make
it almost retroactive,

00:48:13.230 --> 00:48:15.570
as long as you don't do
anything that is contradictory

00:48:15.570 --> 00:48:17.900
to the way that you set
it up in the first book,

00:48:17.900 --> 00:48:18.940
you have the right to do that.

00:48:18.940 --> 00:48:21.050
And it's not that we didn't,

00:48:21.050 --> 00:48:25.690
I do think it was important
with that guy that,

00:48:25.690 --> 00:48:28.500
it does also send the message that

00:48:28.500 --> 00:48:31.680
there are potential allies in this town.

00:48:31.680 --> 00:48:34.120
Everybody's not against these kids.

00:48:34.120 --> 00:48:37.403
It's not just, and Janine, you know?

00:48:38.600 --> 00:48:42.227
So, there's a little bit, I'm
just saying there's a little--

00:48:42.227 --> 00:48:43.900
But I'm just saying that's not the process

00:48:43.900 --> 00:48:46.870
that went into it, it's not the process

00:48:46.870 --> 00:48:50.443
that went into it, but we talked about,

00:48:51.290 --> 00:48:52.640
we haven't talked about
it at all in the show--

00:48:52.640 --> 00:48:55.274
But there's an openness to it,

00:48:55.274 --> 00:48:56.250
that's the point I'm making.

00:48:56.250 --> 00:48:59.053
You know what, and this
leads to another question,

00:49:00.600 --> 00:49:05.477
the question about Dennis,
yes, read that question.

00:49:05.477 --> 00:49:10.477
Julia, which is Jula483 asks,
"Was Dennis calling Janine,

00:49:11.187 --> 00:49:13.827
"who was still in Bleak Creek
just a weird coincidence,

00:49:13.827 --> 00:49:16.897
"or was there something
supernatural happening there?

00:49:16.897 --> 00:49:20.227
"Him calling her all of a
sudden when she was in doubt

00:49:20.227 --> 00:49:22.367
"and not sure if she should stay or leave

00:49:22.367 --> 00:49:24.510
"was almost too perfect."

00:49:24.510 --> 00:49:28.003
And a related question from gryffiest,

00:49:29.034 --> 00:49:31.640
and she actually replied,
he or she actually replied

00:49:31.640 --> 00:49:34.407
to this question, "That,
or I was even thinking

00:49:34.407 --> 00:49:36.057
"at the time that he
might've been paid off

00:49:36.057 --> 00:49:40.597
"or encouraged by some or
one of the cult members

00:49:40.597 --> 00:49:42.127
"to get her to leave town."

00:49:44.610 --> 00:49:47.283
I wish I had a really
awesome answer to this.

00:49:50.718 --> 00:49:53.220
But the answer is, no, it was just

00:49:54.530 --> 00:49:55.810
the best way to tell the story.

00:49:55.810 --> 00:49:57.860
It was dramatic timing.

00:49:57.860 --> 00:50:00.460
It was a little too perfect, well yeah,

00:50:00.460 --> 00:50:02.450
yeah, it might've been
a little too perfect,

00:50:02.450 --> 00:50:05.010
but sometimes you need your
characters to do something

00:50:05.010 --> 00:50:07.880
that will do the perfect
thing for your story.

00:50:07.880 --> 00:50:12.300
Hey, things happen
serendipitously in real life, too.

00:50:12.300 --> 00:50:13.750
Yeah, they do all the time.

00:50:14.870 --> 00:50:17.197
But, which--

00:50:17.197 --> 00:50:19.590
You wanna avoid the cumulative effect

00:50:19.590 --> 00:50:22.800
of a lot of that happening
and driving the story forward.

00:50:22.800 --> 00:50:24.540
We didn't make it, there's not a whole lot

00:50:24.540 --> 00:50:26.910
of super convenient things that happen,

00:50:26.910 --> 00:50:29.210
but there are some
convenient things that happen

00:50:29.210 --> 00:50:32.340
so you don't have to sit
through an unnecessary chapter

00:50:32.340 --> 00:50:33.900
to move the story forward.

00:50:33.900 --> 00:50:36.872
And also, it's our first novel, I'm sure--

00:50:36.872 --> 00:50:38.370
Listen, don't apologize.

00:50:38.370 --> 00:50:42.430
Well, but I'll also say that I read

00:50:42.430 --> 00:50:46.000
well-established writers all the time,

00:50:46.000 --> 00:50:47.627
and I see things that I'm like

00:50:47.627 --> 00:50:52.627
"Whoa, that right there is
so convenient that it's,"

00:50:52.950 --> 00:50:54.970
and I tend to not be very judgmental

00:50:54.970 --> 00:50:55.860
about that kind of thing,

00:50:55.860 --> 00:50:58.590
but I find some things get
too convenient sometimes.

00:50:58.590 --> 00:51:00.840
I don't think this is
necessarily one of 'em,

00:51:00.840 --> 00:51:03.130
but I think it relates
to the question that we

00:51:03.130 --> 00:51:04.730
were answering before which was,

00:51:07.720 --> 00:51:12.720
kinda the way the
intentions and symbolism,

00:51:13.440 --> 00:51:18.440
in fact I was in a classroom last week,

00:51:23.453 --> 00:51:25.210
while I was doing this tour of this place,

00:51:25.210 --> 00:51:30.210
and in the writing classroom,

00:51:31.190 --> 00:51:33.823
the English classroom, there was a,

00:51:35.060 --> 00:51:37.260
I'm gonna get this
wrong, but the quote was,

00:51:39.517 --> 00:51:41.940
"I don't know whether the author intended

00:51:43.227 --> 00:51:45.707
"what my teacher says they intended

00:51:45.707 --> 00:51:47.530
"or if she's just making it up."

00:51:47.530 --> 00:51:49.300
But it was a very funny way

00:51:49.300 --> 00:51:53.640
to kind of word that, and I think that...

00:51:53.640 --> 00:51:56.080
We're just glad to be a
part of the discussion.

00:51:56.080 --> 00:51:57.160
I think it may have been Hank Green

00:51:57.160 --> 00:51:59.510
talking about his book, but
somebody had seen something

00:51:59.510 --> 00:52:00.907
in his book and he was like

00:52:00.907 --> 00:52:03.917
"Oh, I'd like to take credit for that,

00:52:03.917 --> 00:52:08.255
"but no, and I'm glad that
you see that that way."

00:52:08.255 --> 00:52:10.190
But I think it goes
back to the process of,

00:52:10.190 --> 00:52:12.257
once you put yourself in
a certain frame of mind,

00:52:12.257 --> 00:52:14.060
and you're in a certain
setting, and you're telling

00:52:14.060 --> 00:52:17.710
a certain story, there
are going to be things

00:52:17.710 --> 00:52:21.130
that, even the observation that you made

00:52:21.130 --> 00:52:24.580
about the nature of the
friendship and the way

00:52:24.580 --> 00:52:26.630
that they held hands around the tree,

00:52:26.630 --> 00:52:30.990
the observation that you
made in the documentary,

00:52:30.990 --> 00:52:35.220
which wasn't, the reason
we put it in the book

00:52:35.220 --> 00:52:37.620
is because, well that
was what we remembered.

00:52:37.620 --> 00:52:40.070
We remembered three people holding hands

00:52:40.070 --> 00:52:42.030
around a tree and not being able to reach,

00:52:42.030 --> 00:52:44.900
and then you made the
